WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.899
STEWART BRAND: Among economists, to what extent is there a consensus that the house is on

00:00:03.899 --> 00:00:11.460
fire? MARIANA MAZZUCATO: In terms of how Greta [Thunberg] uses it? So climate? BRAND: You say basically it's in

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:16.320
trouble. MAZZUCATO: I mean the problem is that there's this—if the question is amongst

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:20.460
economists, there's a lot of introspection

00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:24.800
about economic theory, where it's gone
wrong. And there's a movement really

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:30.090
about rethinking economics. And there's a bit of a danger there, because economists

00:00:30.090 --> 00:00:34.680
love to show how mathematical and
scientific they are compared to

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:40.920
the sociologists and the anthropologists. And so unfortunately part of the

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:45.149
rethinking economics movement has bought into that and is trying to

00:00:45.149 --> 00:00:48.780
make economics more user-friendly. You
know, the idea is there's too much math, we

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:52.230
need to make it much more about
real life. And the problem is that it's

00:00:52.230 --> 00:00:56.730
not too much math, it's the wrong math.
It's math from Newtonian physics. And

00:00:56.730 --> 00:01:00.420
it's all about equilibria, and
unique equilibria, and averages matter

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:03.960
and the representative agent. There were
all sorts of other mathematical tools

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:06.990
that could have been used, for example,
from biology. In fact, lots of the

00:01:06.990 --> 00:01:10.619
agent-based modeling today—but that's
kind of ghettoized in economics, it's

00:01:10.619 --> 00:01:16.650
not out there at the center—uses more
kind of metaphors and mathematics

00:01:16.650 --> 00:01:22.530
from biology. But also, my thesis actually
was using replicator dynamics,

00:01:22.530 --> 00:01:27.960
distance-from-mean dynamics. In
economics we assume averages.

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:33.180
In biology you couldn't explain
change if everyone looked the same right

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:36.540
distance from mean. You show your
growth by how different you are from me,

00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:39.840
how different your fitness is. And yet
this focus on averages and

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:45.479
representative actors in economics is
fundamentally different from how

00:01:45.479 --> 00:01:49.350
growth and change is looked at in
other sciences. And so I think what's

00:01:49.350 --> 00:01:52.530
really important—and this is what I
guess I was trying to do also in the

00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:58.469
book—is to debunk the
assumptions underlying the models, versus

00:01:58.469 --> 00:02:03.930
to say, get rid of the math. But
in terms of the house being on fire with

00:02:03.930 --> 00:02:07.229
climate change, unfortunately—and this is my point about just seeing it as

00:02:07.229 --> 00:02:10.229
patching up different types of market
failures—it's not that we don't need the

00:02:10.229 --> 00:02:12.989
carbon tax; of course we need carbon
taxes.

00:02:12.989 --> 00:02:17.549
But not really seeing the green
transition and the Green Revolution

00:02:17.549 --> 00:02:22.680
that's required as an active market
co-creation and market shaping process

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:26.790
across that whole system is a lost
opportunity. We should remember that big

00:02:26.790 --> 00:02:31.829
revolutions in the previous waves of
technology, for example mass production,

00:02:31.829 --> 00:02:35.269
would never have had the effect that
they did across the whole economy

00:02:35.269 --> 00:02:40.500
without also really bold demand side
policies. So, suburbanization was

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:44.189
fundamental to the power of mass
production to really affect production

00:02:44.189 --> 00:02:47.340
distribution consumption and
productivity. And what we need today is

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:51.329
the equivalent of these kind of demand-side policies like suburbanization, but

00:02:51.329 --> 00:02:55.200
in a green direction. And we're not
really getting except in some isolated

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:57.470
cases.

